//! Additional explanatory paragraphs for the guide's deep dives.
//! Examples are hypothetical, not conclusions about the current recording.

pub fn source(name: &str) -> Option<[&'static str; 2]> {
    Some(match name {
        "cpu.util" => ["A CPU alternates between idle time and work in states such as user and kernel. Utilization summarizes these intervals across the machine. On a multicore host, one fully busy core can coexist with a low machine-wide percentage; the denominator matters when comparing machines with different core counts.", "For example, a single-threaded server can be limited by one saturated core while the host overall looks mostly idle. Compare the target's CPU time and its busiest thread with runnable wait. A busy CPU can also be doing useful work, so first relate the period to throughput or latency."],
        "cpu.oncpu_time" => ["CPU time accumulates only while a task is scheduled on a processor. If four threads each run concurrently for one second, their combined CPU time can approach four seconds even though one second of wall time elapsed. The recorded scope and aggregation determine which tasks contribute.", "Suppose a request takes two seconds but its thread has only a small amount of CPU time. Reducing instructions on that thread may have limited effect if most elapsed time is spent waiting. Inspect the thread's timebar and runnable delay before choosing an optimization."],
        "cpu.profile" => ["A sampling profiler periodically observes the active call stack. Functions encountered in many samples are candidates for consuming substantial running time. A stack attributes a sample to its callers as well as its leaf; this is why inclusive and own cost answer different questions.", "Imagine a parser appears under several request handlers. A wide common parent may only dispatch work, whereas a leaf with high own cost actually accounts for many samples. Zoom in, check the sample count and missing stacks, and repeat the same workload after a change; short-lived work can be under-sampled."],
        "mem.rss" => ["Virtual memory gives each process an address space, but not every mapped page is currently resident in physical RAM. RSS counts resident pages associated with the process, including pages shared with other processes. It is a footprint, not a direct measure of allocation rate or private ownership.", "A service that grows during warm-up and then stays flat may be retaining a useful cache rather than leaking. Compare equivalent cycles after warm-up; if the baseline keeps rising, investigate allocation and retention data. Also inspect memory pressure: a large resident set can be harmless when sufficient memory remains."],
        "mem.faults" => ["A page fault occurs when a memory access needs the kernel to establish or update a mapping. A minor fault may be satisfied without reading storage, for example when a page is already resident or a new page is mapped. A major fault generally requires waiting for a page to be brought in.", "A burst of faults during startup can be normal as code and data are first touched. If a latency spike coincides with faults, check the available type breakdown and storage activity rather than assuming every fault caused disk I/O. Counts alone do not describe the amount of data transferred or the time spent."],
        "mem.pressure" => ["Memory pressure measures time lost when tasks cannot make progress because the system is dealing with memory constraints. It asks a different question from memory occupancy: cached pages can occupy RAM without causing stalls, while reclaim or swapping can disrupt work when memory is scarce.", "If request latency rises while pressure rises, compare their time windows and inspect reclaim, swap and per-process memory where available. This is evidence for investigation, not proof that the chosen process caused the pressure: the pressure source may cover the whole machine."],
        "io.bytes" => ["Block-device bytes describe traffic at the storage layer. They are affected by the page cache, writeback, filesystem metadata, readahead and other processes. Application reads and writes occur at a different layer, so their byte counts need not match the device's totals in a short window.", "For example, repeated reads served from cache can produce substantial application read traffic with little device traffic. Conversely, delayed writeback can appear after the process issued its writes. Compare the process's operations, the device and a sufficiently aligned time window before attributing traffic."],
        "io.latency" => ["A block request has a duration between the observation points used by its collector. A distribution is more informative than an average when a small number of slow requests can delay user-visible work. A percentile summarizes observations in the chosen population; it is not the duration of every request.", "A p99 increase during a storage burst is a reason to inspect the corresponding period, not proof that storage caused an application's slow request. Check the measurement scope, request volume, lost events and the target's own wait. The heatmap can show whether slow observations cluster in time."],
        "io.syscall" => ["A file I/O syscall is the application's interface to the kernel. Depending on the operation and source configuration, a recorded value may describe duration, bytes or another attribute. The call can complete from cache, wait for storage, or perform other kernel work; the syscall and device layers are not interchangeable.", "If a write call is fast but data appears on disk later, buffering and writeback are possible explanations; a durability operation may have a different cost. Separate operation types and threads, check the selected unit, then compare with block traffic and latency over the same period."],
        "io.fs" => ["File-oriented events add names or file identities to I/O observations when the backend can obtain them. They help connect activity to a file or filesystem operation, but their scope and attribution depend on what was recorded and on how files and devices are represented.", "If a file dominates observed bytes, first check the time window and whether data is complete. A file name alone does not show whether those operations reached a block device or were served from cache. Compare with syscall activity and device traffic using the flows view."],
        "net.bytes" => ["Interface counters summarize traffic at a machine network interface, often separated into receive and transmit directions. These counts include traffic from processes other than the target. Different interfaces and network layers can observe the same logical communication, especially for local or virtual networking.", "If a server's socket writes and interface bytes differ, do not treat the gap as lost application data automatically. Check loopback, protocol overhead, other workloads and the exact interval. Use socket-level observations for the target and interface counters for the machine context."],
        "net.socket" => ["Socket operations show what the target asked the kernel to send or receive and, when captured, how long those operations took. A duration can include waiting for data or buffer space, scheduler effects and kernel work. It is not a direct measurement of wire latency.", "For example, a long receive can simply mean the peer sent data late. Compare operation direction, bytes and request timing; then inspect network-side evidence such as retransmissions in the same window. Keep the recorded value attribute and units visible when comparing rows."],
        "net.tcp.retransmit" => ["TCP retransmits data when its delivery machinery determines another transmission is needed. Retransmissions can occur for several reasons, including loss or timing effects. This measurement's machine-wide scope means a count may include connections unrelated to the selected target.", "A few retransmissions on a busy host are not, by themselves, an explanation for a slow request. Compare the timing and traffic volume with target socket or request observations, and investigate the relevant connection using suitable additional instrumentation before attributing a cause."],
        "net.request" => ["An instrumented application can declare the beginning and end of a request so the recording can group evidence around that unit of work. This makes it possible to compare individual requests rather than relying only on aggregate throughput or average duration.", "If a small group of requests is slow, compare them with faster siblings and inspect what was recorded during each request. The view cannot reconstruct requests the program did not declare, and missing or lost events may leave a request's explanation incomplete."],
        "gpu.engine_busy" => ["GPU engine utilization summarizes activity over the sampling interval reported by the device or driver. A long interval can smooth short kernels and gaps into one average, while device-wide activity may include other processes or engines.", "A low average does not establish that every kernel ran slowly or that the GPU was idle at every instant. Compare the sampled interval and timestamp uncertainty with kernel traces and host-thread activity. Use lanes to investigate the relative order where the recording supports it."],
        "gpu.mem" => ["GPU memory measurements describe memory held at the scope supplied by the backend: a device, a target, or another recorded scope. Allocators can reserve memory for reuse, so held memory does not necessarily equal memory touched by a particular kernel.", "When memory rises, compare allocation phases, workload size and whether the value later stabilizes. A device-wide increase could include another process; verify the label and collection scope before concluding that the target is retaining memory."],
        "gpu.kernel" => ["A GPU kernel executes on the device after work has been submitted by the host. Launches and completion can be asynchronous, and kernels or transfers may overlap depending on the workload and hardware. Device execution duration is therefore only one part of end-to-end work.", "A long kernel may be a worthwhile candidate, but first check whether it is on the critical path of the slow request. Compare its lane with host waits and neighboring kernels, and consider timestamp uncertainty and missing events before inferring overlap."],
        "power.energy" => ["Energy is accumulated over an interval, usually in joules. Average power is energy divided by elapsed time, in watts. A shorter run can consume less energy even at higher power; compare the same amount of completed work as well as elapsed time.", "Hardware counters often describe a package or another physical domain shared by multiple workloads. A difference between runs is not automatically caused by one process. Keep the domain and window consistent, and compare activity and background load before attributing energy."],
        "sched.offcpu" => ["A thread is off CPU whenever it is not executing. Depending on the observed state, that interval may include intentional sleep, waiting for I/O, synchronization or other scheduler states. The source's available stacks and state information help distinguish cases, but do not always capture the full reason.", "A server waiting for the next connection can spend most of its life off CPU while behaving correctly. Focus on the thread and interval that correspond to a slow operation, then use timebar and lanes to separate recorded states and inspect the surrounding events."],
        "sched.runqlat" => ["A runnable thread is ready to execute but may have to wait until the scheduler runs it. Run-queue latency measures this delay at the source's observation points. It differs from time sleeping for I/O or a lock, because the task is eligible to run during the queue wait.", "Suppose response latency rises and the affected thread also shows long runnable waits. CPU competition is a plausible lead, but one spike alone is insufficient. Compare other runnable tasks, per-thread activity and CPU or cgroup limits over the same period; also check losses and source resolution."],
        "sched.futex" => ["Futexes are kernel primitives used by many user-space synchronization mechanisms. A recorded futex operation can reveal part of a thread's synchronization behavior, especially when a lock cannot be acquired immediately, but it is not a complete application-level lock trace.", "A thread waiting in a futex is not enough to identify which lock caused a slow request or who held it. Compare off-CPU stacks, the timebar and wake-up relationships; if available, application-level lock names and ownership provide stronger evidence."],
        "sched.switches" => ["A context switch marks a scheduler transition between tasks. Frequent switching can arise from useful handoffs, blocking I/O, timers, contention or preemption. Counts provide a clue about behavior, but do not encode a complete explanation for each transition.", "Compare switch activity per thread with the workload's normal baseline instead of treating a single count as inherently bad. In lanes, inspect the corresponding intervals alongside off-CPU states and wakeups, then verify whether those intervals affect the work you care about."],
        "sched.wakeup" => ["A wake-up makes a thread runnable; the thread can still wait in a run queue before it actually executes. The recording can associate wake events with wakers, but the captured chain depends on available events and scope, and may omit activity outside the target.", "A repeated handoff can help locate coordination between threads, yet it does not by itself show that the waker caused the entire delay. Compare wake timing, runnable latency and the thread's previous blocked state before selecting a contention hypothesis."],
        _ => return None,
    })
}

pub fn view(name: &str) -> Option<[&'static str; 2]> {
    Some(match name {
        "overview" => ["An overview is a map of evidence, not an automatic diagnosis. Start with a concrete question: for example, which resource changed when response time increased? Select a family and check the scope and quality of the underlying measurement before comparing it with another line.", "A missing value may mean the source was not requested, was refused, or did not observe data. Follow the family into its source table to find which case applies. An advanced view can then help examine timing, attribution or distributions."],
        "system" => ["The system view puts machine-level resource activity near the process list so you can identify an interval and possible participants. CPU, memory, devices and interfaces answer different questions; their apparent peaks do not necessarily belong to the selected process.", "For example, a network peak during a slow process run could be background traffic. Select the process, inspect its ledger and compare target socket data with the machine interface over the same window. Check the collection cost and quality before trusting small differences."],
        "process" => ["The process view compresses several resource families into one timeline-oriented picture. It helps you choose where to investigate, but each row has its own unit and source, and some rows can describe machine-wide activity rather than a process-attributed quantity.", "Suppose memory and network both rise while a request slows. Neither graph establishes causation alone. Open the relevant detail, align the interval with request or thread timing, and distinguish observed values from unavailable or degraded data."],
        "ledger" => ["A ledger groups measured resource costs by category, making it easier to compare the same target or two runs. It helps answer how much was observed in each category, while its quality and collection-cost indicators show what the measurement itself can support.", "Do not add unrelated quantities such as bytes, elapsed time and CPU time as though they were one budget. Compare matching rows under equivalent work, then open a detail view to determine where a difference came from and whether attribution is complete."],
        "flame" => ["A flame view arranges call stacks as nested frames. Frame width represents the recorded weight of a stack, not chronological position: adjacent frames need not have executed one after another. On-CPU samples and off-CPU duration are separate measures with separate units.", "A wide parent includes the work of its descendants. To find code that itself consumes time, inspect own cost and the leaves; to understand why that code ran, inspect its callers. Sampling, incomplete stacks and symbolization can change the visible picture. Compare equivalent recordings before claiming an improvement."],
        "request" => ["A request view groups recorded evidence around declared units of application work. Comparing many requests can reveal whether a slow tail is shared by all requests or concentrated in a subset, such as a particular phase or resource wait.", "An example workflow is to select a slow request, compare it with a faster sibling, then follow its threads into timebar and lanes. The result is constrained by the application's request instrumentation and by event quality: absent evidence is not automatically absent work."],
        "timebar" => ["Timebar partitions observed elapsed time into categories such as running, runnable and blocked states. The relative widths help prioritize an investigation, but they are aggregate amounts rather than a chronological recording of which state came first.", "If a thread spends most time sleeping, ask whether it is waiting for work normally or blocking a slow request. Use lanes to see when intervals happened and stacks or wakeups to investigate why. Unknown or overlapping measurements must not be silently treated as exact components."],
        "heatmap" => ["A latency heatmap places observations into time and duration buckets. Horizontal bands can suggest repeated regimes, while isolated high cells can reveal a tail hidden by the mean. The chart summarizes observed events rather than displaying every request independently.", "A new slow band is an investigation starting point, not a diagnosis. Select the relevant period, check bucket resolution and loss indicators, then compare that same window with request, scheduler and I/O evidence. Sparse observations and aggregation can make patterns look more certain than they are."],
        "lanes" => ["Lanes align observed thread or GPU-stream activity on a common time axis. They are useful for seeing overlap, gaps and handoffs that disappear when durations are summed into a single total.", "At a wide zoom, a character can summarize many events, so apparent adjacency does not guarantee exact ordering. Zoom into the interval of interest, check timestamp uncertainty and missing events, then follow the selected thread into its timebar or wake chain."],
        "wake" => ["The wake view follows observed relationships that make threads runnable. A repeated chain may show how work is handed between threads or where an external event enters the recorded scope.", "Wake-up and execution are distinct moments: a thread can be awake and still wait for CPU scheduling. A missing waker may be outside the recording. Compare the chain with run-queue delay and lanes before treating one relationship as the cause of a slow operation."],
        "flows" => ["Flows compares measured bytes across layers such as a process, its sockets or files, and machine interfaces or devices. Differences can reveal useful questions about buffering, caching, loopback and attribution.", "A difference between two columns is not automatically loss. The layers may cover different scopes or windows, count overhead differently, or include background work. Check those assumptions and follow the relevant file or socket before assigning a cause."],
        "family-cpu" => ["CPU sources answer different questions: utilization describes machine activity, on-CPU time accounts for running tasks, and profiling samples where that time was spent. Their scopes and denominators must be checked before comparing them.", "Start with the period of poor throughput or latency. Compare target CPU time with runnable wait, then inspect a profile if the target is actively running. A busy machine alone does not identify which function or process to change."],
        "family-memory" => ["Memory usage, faults and pressure describe different phenomena. Resident memory is a footprint, faults are mapping events, and pressure describes stalled progress. Looking at only one can make normal caching look like a failure or hide actual stalls.", "Compare the workload's phases with RSS and pressure. If a baseline grows, investigate allocation and retention across equivalent cycles; if pressure rises, inspect reclaim or swap evidence where available. Shared memory and machine-wide sources require careful attribution."],
        "family-disk" => ["The disk family joins measurements from application syscalls and the block-device layer. The page cache, filesystem and writeback sit between these observations, so counts and timings may differ even for correct behavior.", "Compare reads and writes, request latency and the workload's slow window. Use the heatmap for changes in latency distribution and flows for observed paths; check source scope and loss before attributing a device event to one process."],
        "family-network" => ["A network investigation can involve request timing, socket operations and machine interface counters. These are different layers: a long receive can include waiting for a peer, while an interface counter includes traffic from the entire machine.", "Begin with the affected request or socket and compare the same interval with interface traffic and retransmissions. Local traffic, virtual interfaces and protocol overhead complicate byte comparisons, so use differences as questions rather than proof of loss."],
        "family-gpu" => ["GPU utilization, held memory and kernel duration describe different parts of a workload. Device-wide samples can include other processes and smooth short bursts; target kernel events have their own scope and timestamp quality.", "Compare the host thread and device lanes around a slow phase. Determine whether a kernel is on the critical path before optimizing it, and verify that the observed device activity belongs to the target."],
        "family-energy" => ["Energy adds power over time. Comparing watts can reveal a change in instantaneous or average power, while joules per completed unit of work can reveal a change in energy efficiency.", "Keep workload, duration and hardware domain comparable. Package or device counters can include work outside the target, so compare resource activity and background load before attributing the difference."],
        "family-time" => ["Elapsed time can be spent running, waiting to be scheduled, sleeping or blocked. These categories answer different questions and often need several sources to reconstruct. A thread with little CPU time is not necessarily fast.", "Find the thread or request that matters, use timebar for the aggregate split, then lanes for timing and wake for observed handoffs. Intentional waiting is normal in many systems; investigate whether the wait lies on the path of the work being measured."],
        _ => return None,
    })
}
